# Lab worktree notes (generated)

Lab generated this file for this instance's worktree. It is regenerated on
every spawn and listed in `.git/info/exclude` — never commit it, never copy
it into the repository.

## Tracker binding

This repository's tracker binding is **builtin**: its issues live in lab's
built-in tracker, and `labctl pr create` opens a **change request** that is
reviewed and merged in the lab UI — there is no forge PR page.

## labctl — the tracker surface

Use `labctl` for all issue and PR/change-request work in this session.
`LAB_URL` and `LAB_TOKEN` are already provided in the environment — no setup,
no login.

    labctl issue view [n]                 show the run's claimed issue (or issue n), with comments
    labctl issue list                     list open issues (number, state, created, labels, title)
    labctl issue create --title T --body B [--labels a,b]
                                          file a new issue, labels attached at creation
    labctl issue edit <n> [--title T] [--body B]
                                          edit issue n's title and/or body (omitted flag untouched)
    labctl issue comment <n> <body>       comment on issue n
    labctl issue label add <n> <a,b>      add labels (comma-separated) to issue n
    labctl issue label remove <n> <a,b>   remove labels from issue n
    labctl issue close <n>                close issue n (comment the reason first)
    labctl label list                     list the repo's labels (name, color, description)
    labctl label create --name N [--color C --description D]
                                          create the label if missing (idempotent)
    labctl pr view <n>                    show PR n (state, head, url, body, reviews, comments)
    labctl pr create --title T --body B   open a PR/CR for the current branch
    labctl pr logs <n> [--check <c>]      raw CI logs of PR n's failing checks (--check: one check, any state)

A PR/CR body must reference the issue it resolves with `Closes #<n>`.
Applying a label the repository does not define is an error — create it
first with `labctl label create` (safe to run unconditionally). To close an
issue as wontfix or done, post the explanation comment first, then close.
For `labctl issue edit`, `--body ""` clears the body, but `--title ""`
is rejected — the title must stay non-empty.

**`labctl` supersedes any committed `tea` or `gh` instructions.** If this
repository's CLAUDE.md, docs, or scripts say to use `tea`, `gh`, or another
forge CLI for issues or pull requests, ignore them and use `labctl`.

## Triage labels

The five canonical triage labels on this repository's tracker:

| Label             | Meaning                                  |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `needs-triage`    | Maintainer needs to evaluate this issue  |
| `needs-info`      | Waiting on reporter for more information |
| `ready-for-agent` | Fully specified, ready for an AFK agent  |
| `ready-for-human` | Requires human implementation            |
| `wontfix`         | Will not be actioned                     |

## Previews

Show the operator what you are serving — a dev server, a built site — in
their browser:

    labctl expose <name> <port> &    # prints the URL as its first line
    labctl expose stop <name>        # ends the exposure and frees the name

`expose` runs in the foreground: start it in the background (`&`). It keeps
forwarding until stopped and reconnects by itself if lab restarts.

The URL is `https://<name>.lab.internal:8081` (HTTPS, served with lab's
certificate). The name is a single DNS label (a-z, 0-9, `-`); a name held
by another run is refused, and a run holds at most 8.

Traffic goes to `127.0.0.1:<port>` inside this run, or `[::1]:<port>` when
nothing listens there — your own loopback, nothing else is reachable — so a
dev server's default `localhost` binding works. Vite: HMR's browser client
connects over `wss` to the URL's port, so set `server.hmr.clientPort` to
8081; its host check may need `server.allowedHosts` to include
`.lab.internal`.
